    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d43">

                <pb facs="0151" n="61" sameAs="frus:frus1934v03/pg61"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">893.01 Manchuria/1019: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Secretary of State to the Chargé in Guatemala</hi>
                        (<hi rendition="#italic">Lawton</hi>)<anchor n="70" xml:id="d43-f1"/></head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1934-03-05T17:00:00-05:00">March 5, 1934—5 p.m.</date></dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>16. Your telegram No. 20, March 2, 4 p.m.<anchor n="71" xml:id="d43-f2"/> You may
                    inform the Minister for Foreign Affairs orally that the Department has received
                    a similar telegram, that the American Government’s policy of nonrecognition of
                    Manchukuo remains unchanged and that all messages received here from that source
                    are filed without acknowledgment.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Hull</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d43-f1" n="70" type="source" xml:id="d43-f1-content">
                <p>Similar telegrams were sent to the American missions in Ecuador, Nicaragua, and
                    Honduras.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d43-f2" n="71" xml:id="d43-f2-content">
                <p>Not printed.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
